you  so one of the questions I get asked all  the time is when you're out in public or  in your the real world when you're  having dinner with friends and you're  meeting your neighbors or relatives what  do you do if evolution comes up as a  topic of conversation or somebody says I  don't believe in evolution or I don't  think they should teach it in the  schools or it's just a theory  well we're in your right up front this  is a really good way to Elliott people  and get them upset with you but I think  there are much better ways to handle  disagreement and one of the first things  that I try to do when someone brings  that point of view up is politely  indicate that I disagree with them so  they understand what's going to follow  but then I try to do something that I  think all of you at this stage in your  careers can do very effectively and that  is what I'm not how science works  there's a stereotype out in the real  world there's a stereotype that science  is kind of like a closed community real  tight-knit fraternity or sorority we're  getting ahead depends on doing exactly  what your superiors want and always  cowering up to the big people the  National Academy people department chair  stuff like that the reality as I'm sure  all of you know is that science works in  an entirely different way the worst  thing anybody could hope for embarking  on a research project are results that  simply confirm existing ideas that's  boring what we all lay awake at night  dreaming about is the revolutionary  discovery the one that's going to upset  the applecart the one that's going to  destroy the common theory because that's  how you can make your reputation nobody  ever got famous by confirming other  ideas they got famous by coming up with  revolutionary results and hatching new  ideas of their own emphasized that  that's how science works the same  dynamic trust me  works in the field of evolutionary  biology at the genetics and the  molecular level the way in which people  get ahead is by testing by looking by  probing the theory any theory for  weaknesses and that counts as evolution  as well that counts in evolution as well  and what I first try to do is let people  know that science is contentious that  the very dynamic of science the  competitive nature of science guarantees  that if evolution was a uniquely weak or  shaky theory with no evidence behind it  people be standing in line scientists  standing a line to knock it off its  pedestal they're not I can't think of a  single theory in the life sciences that  has been tested and probed more  thoroughly than evolution it's a hundred  and fifty two years after the  publication of the Origin of Species and  it's still standing that's a darn good  theory second point that I try to make  is what a theory in science means to an  average person theory is just a guess or  a hunch  like I have a theory as to why the San  Francisco Giants won the pennant last  year or I have a theory as to why this  particular politicians ideas are good or  bad or indifferent it's not what we mean  by theory in science many people think  that fact is something you're certain  about in theory something shaky the  reality is that theory is a higher level  of understanding than fact and here's  the reason why  facts are simply repeatable isolated  observations I drop this ball it falls  down Sun comes up in the morning the  wavelength of light visible light is the  following these are all facts what  theories do is to take collections of  isolated facts and put them together  into a single explanatory framework for  example the theory the atomic theory of  matter is a theory that literally takes  hundreds of thousands of facts  observations about the nature of matter  and ties them all together into an  explanatory theory none of us in  education ever explained that expects a  course in atomic theory ten years from  now to be called of course in atomic  facts be as if atoms were something we  weren't sure about theories in science  never become facts theories in science  explain facts and the reality is but  although people raised a lot of  arguments against evolution there isn't  a single experimental factor observation  that has appeared in the last 150 years  that contradicts the broad outlines of  the theory of evolution then the last  thing I try to do is really very simple  and that is to say  science is an enterprise in which we try  to find out how the natural world works  it's not an enterprise that takes on  religion in a hostile way in fact many  of the greatest scientists in all  history have in fact been deeply  religious and we can start with Galileo  and Newton but in more recent times  gregor mendel the founder of the modern  science of genetics he was a priest as  it turns out theodosius dobzhansky i  know it's a big name but he's probably  the greatest evolutionary geneticist of  the 20th century he was a devout person  as well Francis Collins terrific cell  and molecular biologist who heads the  National Institutes of Health as an  evangelical Christian all of the people  that I just mentioned the recent people  are in fact strong supporters and  contributors to the science of evolution  now people can look at evolution and  draw a variety of conclusions as to how  it fits the religious faith but  evolutionary science itself like all of  science really is neutral on issues of  religion there are wastes as understand  evolution within a religious context and  I've actually written about those in a  couple of books and many articles and  other scientists have to and finally and  here's the most important thing when  someone expresses disagreement with you  on a point this fundamental I think what  you have to do is engage them and rather  than argue with him and by engage I mean  engage them as you would a colleague if  you and your colleague are working on  the same problem and you have slightly  different ideas about it what do you do  do you call each other names well maybe  in some cases in some fields you do but  what you do ideally is you lay out your  data you show each other your chell's  your sequences your atomic level  structures and from this airing of data  the other person begins to see that  you're a person of goodwill that you're  just interested in solving the same  problem that they are I have found that  technique in effect trying to take  people in to the scientific community  saying well let me show you why we think  this is so to be enormous ly effective  now I'll guarantee you you're not going  to be familiar with every argument that  comes out against the theory of  evolution some of your colleagues well  you might want to run though  arguments down you might want to go to  websites to try to find answers to these  arguments there's a really good website  called talk origins t ALK ori GI n s  talk origins calm in which all of the  anti evolution arguments that i've ever  heard of are answered in a very  definitive way so there are resources  out there for you but i think the most  important thing that any of us can do as  a scientist or as an apprentice  scientist is to let people know why we  went into science it wasn't because we  have a secular humanist axe to grind  it's not because we want to destroy god  it's because we really really want to  understand how the world works and when  people see that sincere motivation when  they see the twinkle in your eye you're  going to win them over they might be a  little baffled by some of the science  but once they see as i say that gleam in  your eye the the glint the excitement of  scientific discovery they're gonna drop  their suspicions of science and they're  gonna appreciate you as just another  person working in this great enterprise  lot's of luck with that  you

